<div class="meta">Lesson 0010 · Multiplayer networking · from feat(net): route NPC dialogue through the server</div>
<h1>Request-Id Routing: One LLM, Many Askers</h1>
<p>How <code>HostChatRouter</code> gives four players one shared NPC brain without
crossing anyone's wires.</p>

<h2>The fan-in / fan-out problem</h2>
<p>All NPC replies stream out of a single <code>LlmClient</code> as anonymous-looking
<code>ChatDelta{id, text}</code> fragments. In solo play, "who is this for?" had one easy
answer: the open dialogue. With remote players, one stream now serves:</p>
<ul>
<li>the host's own conversation (rendered in the local <code>DialogUI</code>),</li>
<li>player 2 chatting with the baker (goes over player 2's socket <em>only</em>),</li>
<li>player 3 chatting with the cop (player 3's socket <em>only</em>).</li>
</ul>
<p>The routing key already existed: <code>LlmClient::submit()</code> returns a unique,
monotonically increasing <strong>request id</strong>, and every delta and reply carries it.
The router just remembers who each id belongs to:</p>
<pre><code>std::unordered_map&lt;uint64_t, std::pair&lt;int,int&gt;&gt; inflight_;  // id → {playerId, npcIndex}

uint64_t id = npc.ask(line.text);          // submit through the SHARED path
inflight_[id] = {line.playerId, npcIndex}; // claim the id for that player</code></pre>

<h2>Consume-or-pass: chaining drains without forking them</h2>
<p>The host's frame loop drains the LLM queues exactly once, offering each item to
the router first. <em>Consumed</em> means "that was a remote player's"; <em>declined</em>
falls through to the existing solo handling, untouched:</p>
<pre><code>for (auto&amp; d : client.drainDeltas()) {
    if (chatRouter &amp;&amp; chatRouter-&gt;routeDelta(d)) continue;  // remote → socket
    if (session.deltaArrived(d.id, d.text))                 // mine → DialogUI
        dialog.appendStreamingDelta(d.text);
}</code></pre>
<div class="callout good">This boolean consume-or-pass shape is how you extend a
single-consumer queue to two consumers without rewriting either: the new consumer
gets first refusal, the old code cannot tell anything changed.</div>

<h2>Serializing a shared NPC</h2>
<p>An <code>Npc</code> tracks exactly one pending request (<code>pendingId_</code>), so two
simultaneous questions to the same NPC would clobber each other. The router
enforces one-at-a-time <em>per NPC</em> with a queue:</p>
<pre><code>queued_[npcIndex].push_back({playerId, text});   // arrival order
// pumpNpc(): if (!npc.waiting()) submit the front line</code></pre>
<p><code>npc.waiting()</code> covers <em>anyone's</em> in-flight question — including the host's —
so host and guests can't interleave a conversation turn. When a reply resolves,
the router pumps that NPC's queue again: the next asker gets their turn, in
order. Different NPCs don't queue on each other (the single LlmClient worker
serializes actual HTTP calls, but that's an implementation detail the router
doesn't depend on).</p>

<h2>Private stream, public consequences</h2>
<p>On a successful reply the router does three sends with different audiences:</p>
<ol>
<li><code>ChatReply</code> → <strong>the asker only</strong> (their transcript).</li>
<li><code>NpcSpeechBubble</code> → <strong>everyone</strong> (bystanders see the NPC speak).</li>
<li><code>NpcMoodUpdate</code> → <strong>everyone</strong> (faces update on all screens).</li>
</ol>
<p>Failures are the asymmetric case: an "Ollama unreachable" error goes to the
requesting player <em>only</em>. Broadcasting errors would tell every player about a
problem only one of them can act on.</p>
<div class="callout">Note the shared-memory consequence: <code>npc.onReplyArrived()</code> appends to
the one true history no matter who asked. Tell the baker your name, and she knows
it when your friend talks to her — that's the "same NPC for everyone" feature,
implemented by <em>not</em> duplicating state.</div>

<h2>Disconnects: drop the route, keep the world</h2>
<p>If a player leaves mid-reply, the reply still resolves against the NPC (history
stays consistent), but <code>sendToPlayer()</code> returns false and the payload evaporates.
Nothing else notices. The loopback test proves the next player can immediately use
the same NPC — the failure of one client is never allowed to wedge shared state.</p>
